[36m(RolloutManager pid=22940)[0m [2026-10-09 17:59:40] sglang_rollout.py:602 - eval_rollout_single_dataset example data: ['A security engineer on your team has asked you to perform a **credential-rotation drill** for three internal services that are configured by simple “*.cfg*” files located under */home/user/infra/*.\nThe engineer wants the rotation performed according to a very strict pattern so that the company’s compliance automation can later verify that the change happened exactly as expected.\n\nCurrent state (already present when the session starts):\n  • /home/user/infra/db.cfg\n  • /home/user/infra/redis.cfg\n  • /home/user/infra/api.cfg\n\nEvery one of those files already contains exactly two newline-terminated key/value pairs in the order shown below:\n    username=<service-specific-fixed-value>\n    password=<service-specific-fixed-value>\n\n### Rotation Rule\nYou MUST apply the following rotation rule to the *.cfg* files:\n   • Find the existing *password* value.\n   • Replace the four-digit substring “2022” with “2025”.\n     (Nothing else about the password string changes – you are literally doing a single, deterministic text substitution.)\n   • Leave the *username* line completely untouched.\n\n### Consolidated JSON File\nAfter you finish editing the three *.cfg* files, create a single consolidated JSON file named\n       /home/user/infra/credentials.json\nthat looks **exactly** like this (pay attention to spacing, newlines and quoting because the test harness will check it byte-for-byte):\n\n    {\n      "db":    { "username": "db_user",    "password": "db_pass_2025" },\n      "redis": { "username": "redis_user", "password": "redis_pass_2025" },\n      "api":   { "username": "api_user",   "password": "api_pass_2025" }\n    }\n\nNotes:\n   • The keys must be in the precise order db, redis, api (the harness is strict).\n   • Exactly two spaces before each key inside the JSON object.\n   • Exactly one space after every colon.\n   • Exactly one space after every comma that separates fields.\n   • The file must end with a single trailing newline (Unix LF).\n\n### Audit Trail\nProduce an audit trail.\n   • Append (or create if it does not yet exist) a CSV log file at\n         /home/user/rotation.log\n   • The file **must not** have a header row.\n   • Add **exactly three** new lines, one per rotated credential, in this order:\n\n        db.cfg,db_pass_2022,db_pass_2025\n        redis.cfg,redis_pass_2022,redis_pass_2025\n        api.cfg,api_pass_2022,api_pass_2025\n\n     Each line must terminate with a single LF and there must be no blank line at the end of the file.\n\n### Final State\nWhen everything is complete, the following commands should produce the specified results (these commands are only shown here so that *you* understand the final state – do **not** hard-code their output):\n      cat /home/user/infra/db.cfg      → shows the rotated password containing “2025”.\n      cat /home/user/infra/credentials.json  → exactly matches the JSON block above.\n      cat /home/user/rotation.log      → contains the three CSV lines above (plus any historical lines if the file existed before).\n\n### Constraints\nYou do **not** need root privileges. All work happens in */home/user/*. Do everything from the shell. The end state must comply *exactly* with the requirements for the configuration files, the JSON output, and the audit trail.\n\nThe automation that grades your work will compare the contents of /home/user/infra/credentials.json and /home/user/rotation.log exactly, so follow the required spacing, ordering, and line-terminator conventions precisely.<action>done</action>'] reward=0.0
