nd with a single trailing newline (i.e. a final “\\n” after the last line).  \n        5. You may not manually type every file name; your solution should be able to handle any number of `.log` files in the directory.\n    </requirements>\n\n    <verification>\n        Automated tests will check:\n\n        a. No plain “*.log” files remain in /home/user/support-logs/.  \n        b. gzip archives with matching “.log.gz” names exist for ticket_123, ticket_124 and ticket_125.  \n        c. /home/user/support-logs/compression_summary.txt exists, is a regular text file, contains exactly three lines, each line strictly matching the pattern:\n\n             ^ticket_12[345]\\.log -> ticket_12[345]\\.log\\.gz$\n\n        d. The file ends with a newline character.  \n        e. README.txt remains unchanged.\n    </verification>\n\n    <completion>\n        Respond “DONE” (without additional commentary) when the task is finished so the automatic grader can proceed.\n    </completion><action>done</action>'], label: task_000000_c2332e70, reward: 0.0
