nFAILED_BUILD_IDS:<single space><value>\n```\n\nExample (illustrative only):\n```\nTOTAL_BUILDS: 4\nSUCCESSFUL_BUILDS: 3\nFAILED_BUILDS: 1\nAVERAGE_DURATION_MS: 82625\nFAILED_BUILD_IDS: c1348\n```\n\nThe second report file, `/home/user/mobile_ci/reports/parse_detail.log`, is for later debugging. It must list, **one line per source file**, the file name followed by “→” and the extracted build-id, final status, and duration separated by commas. For example:\n\n```\nbuild_2023-08-01_c1345.log → c1345,SUCCESS,85000\n```\n\nThe `parse_detail.log` file may contain any number of lines but must end with a newline character.\n\n### Constraints\n*   You are limited to typical Unix command-line utilities available on a minimal Debian/Ubuntu system.\n*   Do not alter, move or delete the original log files.\n*   Make sure `/home/user/mobile_ci/reports/` and the two report files are created if they do not already exist.\n\n### Completion criteria (checked automatically)\n1. The file `/home/user/mobile_ci/reports/weekly_summary_2023-08-01_to_2023-08-07.log` exists and is readable by the current user.\n2. The file contains exactly 5 lines and matches the format specified above.\n3. Numerical values in that file correctly reflect the information contained in EVERY `*.log` file under `/home/user/mobile_ci/logs/`.\n4. The file `/home/user/mobile_ci/reports/parse_detail.log` exists, is not empty, and each of its lines follows the required pattern “`<source_file>` → `<build-id>`,<status>,<duration>”.<action>done</action>'], label: task_000000_21013e33, reward: 0.0
