d artefact listed below must occupy one line in that file.\nThe format of **each line is EXACTLY**:\n\n    <relative_path_from_/home/user/backup-work>,<sha256_hex>,<file_size_bytes>\n\n- Use comma (`,`) as the single delimiter; no spaces.\n- `<relative_path…>` must match what is stored in the tarball, e.g.\n  `rootCA.key` or `bundle/rootCA.crt`.\n- `<sha256_hex>` must be the full lower-case 64-character SHA-256 digest.\n- `<file_size_bytes>` is the exact byte count of the file.\n- Sort the five lines in straightforward lexicographic order by the relative path.\n- Ensure the file ends with a single trailing newline character.\n\nWhen you are done, the final directory layout must look like this (⇢ = “contains”):\n\n/home/user/backup-work ⇢  rootCA.key\n                       |  rootCA.crt\n                       |  server.key\n                       |  server.csr\n                       |  server.crt\n                       └─ bundle ⇢  rootCA.crt\n                                   server.crt\n\n/home/user/archives ⇢   cert-backup-20240323.tar.gz\n                       cert-backup-20240323.manifest.csv\n\n### VERIFICATION HINTS\n- The grading robot will untar /home/user/archives/cert-backup-20240323.tar.gz into a temp folder, calculate fresh SHA-256 digests for each file it finds there, and cross-check them against the values in the manifest.\n- Any deviation in file names, date stamp, column order, delimiter, digest length or missing trailing newline will be treated as a failure.\n\nYour task is complete when both the tarball and its manifest exist with the exact names shown above and the manifest passes the checker’s integrity test.<command>mkdir -p /home/user/backup-work/bundle</command>\n<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nCommand executed successfully. Output: \n\n(exit_code=0)<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n<command>openssl genrsa -out /home/user/backup-work/rootCA.key 2048</command>\n<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nCommand executed successfu